Deadlock Avoidance

Before this, we studied:

Deadlock Prevention

which says:

"Don't allow deadlock conditions to happen."

But prevention is often too strict.

So OS designers asked:

Can we allow processes to request resources normally, but intelligently avoid dangerous situations?

Answer:

Deadlock Avoidance
Core Idea

Imagine driving a car.

Prevention says:

Never drive on roads where accidents can happen.

Very restrictive.

Avoidance says:

Drive normally,
but don't enter dangerous situations.

Much smarter.

Definition

Deadlock Avoidance is a strategy in which the OS analyzes resource requests and grants them only if the system remains in a safe state.

The Most Important Concept

Everything in avoidance revolves around:

Safe State

and

Unsafe State
What is a Safe State?

A state where there exists at least one order of process execution such that every process can finish successfully.

Easy Definition

A safe state means:

The system can still complete all processes.

No deadlock will occur.

Real-Life Example

Imagine:

You have:

₹1000

in your wallet.

Your friends may ask:

Friend A → ₹200
Friend B → ₹300
Friend C → ₹400

You can satisfy everyone.

Safe.

Unsafe State

Unsafe does NOT mean deadlock has already happened.

This is extremely important.

Many students think:

Unsafe State = Deadlock

Wrong.

Correct relationship:

Safe State
      ↓
Unsafe State
      ↓
Deadlock

Meaning:

Safe ≠ Deadlock

Unsafe ≠ Deadlock

But Deadlock can arise from Unsafe State.
Analogy

Suppose:

You have:

₹1000

You lend:

₹900

to someone.

Only:

₹100

left.

Nothing bad has happened yet.

But now you are in danger.

This is:

Unsafe State
Safe Sequence

The most important term in Deadlock Avoidance.

A Safe Sequence is:

An order in which all processes can complete.

Example:

P1
↓
P3
↓
P2
↓
P4

If all can finish in that order:

Safe Sequence Exists

Then:

Safe State
Example of Safe State

Available resources:

10

Processes:

P1 needs 3
P2 needs 4
P3 needs 2

OS can schedule:

P3 finishes
↓
returns resources

P1 finishes
↓
returns resources

P2 finishes

Safe sequence exists.

Safe state.

Example of Unsafe State

Suppose:

Available resources:

1

Processes:

P1 needs 2
P2 needs 3
P3 needs 4

Nobody can finish.

System enters risky territory.

Unsafe.

Key Difference
Safe State	Unsafe State
Safe sequence exists	No guaranteed safe sequence
Deadlock impossible	Deadlock possible
OS grants request	OS may deny request
How OS Avoids Deadlock

Whenever a process requests a resource:

OS asks:

"If I grant this request,
will system remain safe?"

If:

YES

Grant request.

If:

NO

Make process wait.

This is Deadlock Avoidance.

Real-Life Example

Bank Manager.

Suppose bank has:

₹1 Crore

Customers request loans.

Bank doesn't blindly approve.

Bank checks:

Can I still satisfy all customers later?

If yes:

Loan Approved

If no:

Loan Denied

This is exactly why the next algorithm is called:

Banker's Algorithm

Designed by Edsger W. Dijkstra.

Difference: Prevention vs Avoidance
Prevention	Avoidance
Break Coffman conditions	Keep Coffman conditions
Very restrictive	More flexible
Simple	More complex
Lower utilization	Better utilization
Deadlock impossible	Deadlock avoided intelligently
Prevention Example

Rule:

Request all resources at once.

Problem:

Resources may sit idle.

Avoidance Example

Rule:

Request when needed.

OS checks safety first.

Better utilization.

Requirements for Deadlock Avoidance

OS must know in advance:

Maximum Resource Need

Example:

P1 may need at most 10 units.

OS needs this information before allocation.

Without it:

Avoidance impossible.
Advantages
Better Resource Utilization

Resources aren't unnecessarily reserved.

More Concurrency

Processes run more freely.

Deadlocks Avoided

Without excessive restrictions.

Disadvantages
Complex

Lots of calculations.

Need Maximum Resource Information

Programs must declare needs beforehand.

Not Practical Everywhere

Modern general-purpose OSes rarely use full avoidance.

Interview Questions
Q1. What is Deadlock Avoidance?

Answer:

A strategy that grants resource requests only if the resulting state remains safe.

Q2. What is a Safe State?

Answer:

A state in which a safe sequence exists and all processes can complete.

Q3. What is an Unsafe State?

Answer:

A state where no guaranteed safe sequence exists and deadlock may occur.

Q4. Is Unsafe State equal to Deadlock?

Answer:

No.

Unsafe state only means deadlock is possible.

Q5. What is a Safe Sequence?

Answer:

An order of process execution in which every process can complete successfully.
